Proof. The bijection ϕ between Fk (P ) and J (P × [k]) will be a natural one, where ϕ(f ) = I
implies f (x) counts the number of elements in the intersection of I and the fiber at x in
P × [k]. That is, the intersection of the fiber at x in P × [k], {(x, 1), (x, 2), . . . , (x, k)}, and an
order ideal I is {(x, 1), (x, 2), . . . , (x, d)} for some d ∈ [k], if and only if f (x) = d. Otherwise,
the intersection is empty if and only if f (x) = 0.
  We will now complete the proof by fixing f and x and showing that
                                 ϕ(wx (f )) = τ(x,1) τ(x,2) . . . τ(x,k) (ϕ(f )).
   Let m = max({f (y) : y ⋖ x, for y ∈ P } ∪ {0}) and M = min({f (y) : x ⋖ y, for y ∈
P } ∪ {k}), so m ≤ f (x) ≤ M . There are two cases:
       • Case 1. If f (x) < M , then (wx (f ))(x) = f (x) + 1.
       • Case 2. If f (x) = M , then f (x) + 1 is greater than M so we increment through
         values modulo k + 1 until we get to (wx (f ))(x) = m.
   On the other hand, we consider the action of τ = τ(x,1) τ(x,2) . . . τ(x,k) on ϕ(f ). Recall that
τ(x,i) does not change the order ideal whenever (x, i) is not maximal in the order ideal or
minimal in the complement of the order ideal. Because of this, there are only two outcomes
of τ (S(f )), either one element was added to order ideal, or elements were removed until
removing anymore would not result in an order ideal. This reflects the two cases above,
which completes the proof.                                                                        □
   The following general theorem is extremely useful for studying the dynamics of rowmo-
tion for certain kinds of posets. Such an equivariant bijection was used by Haddadan [6,
Lemma 3.1] in the specific situation she studied of rowmotion and promotion on triangular
and rectangular posets, but has not been stated in anything like this generality in literature
we have seen.
Theorem 2.12. Fix any linear extension (x1 , x2 , . . . , xp ) ∈ L(P ). There is an equivariant
bijection between Fk (P ) and J (P × [k]) which sends whirling, w = wx1 wx2 · · · wxp on Fk (P )
to rowmotion on J (P × [k]).
Proof. The bijection is based on the one from Lemma 2.11. We will construct the linear
extension that traverses P × [k] fiber by fiber, i.e., (xi , j) → xk(i−1)+j . Then the composite
map τx1 τx2 · · · τxkp on P × [k] coincides with rowmotion by Proposition 1.5. By Lemma 2.11,
8                                          PLANTE AND ROBY

the toggles τxk(i−1)+1 τxk(i−1)+2 · · · τxk(i−1)+k coincide with wxi . Together these give us that w =
wx1 wx2 · · · wxp , is exactly rowmotion.                                                            □
Example 2.13. Figure 2 shows a linear extension of the poset, V and the corresponding
linear extension for V × [4] on the right, as in the proof of Theorem 2.12, of V × [4].
   The following definitions will allow us to partition orbit boards of whirling into subsets
called whorms. The partitioning of orbit boards of combinatorial actions into some special
class of disjoint subsets is a useful tool, used by several authors, including different kinds of
“snakes” [6, 10], and “chunks” [9].
